# The hull of two intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface"): the
# smaller lower bound and the larger upper bound, exact. No test of emptiness: the empty set is
# stored as (+inf, -inf), so the hull with it is the other operand, and of two empty sets the empty
# set (milestone2.md, "Values of an interval"). The bounds are canonical, so no comparison sees a
# NaN. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_hull_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       interval_set zl zu union x=xl,xu y=yl,yu
min_precision: 1
source:        the smaller lower bound and the larger upper bound (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalExact.v interval_hull_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the bounds are selected among the bounds of the operands; interval_set states what comes out for every pair of canonical intervals

ml = lt(yl, xl)
zl = select(ml, yl, xl)
mu = lt(xu, yu)
zu = select(mu, yu, xu)
